WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:02.680
Cardiovascular disease

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.450
and specifically heart failure

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.040
is the number one cause of death

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.690
in men and women across just
about every demographic.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.820
If we had a patient with a bad heart

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.000
and we could take their heart out

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.703
and replace it with a machine,
we'd do it all the time.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.160
For the last half century,

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.070
scientists have been working
tirelessly to create a machine

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.870
that can accurately mimic the function

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.690
of a beating human heart,

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:25.900
envisioning devices

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.490
they hope will one day rid us

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.843
of the number one cause
of death in the world.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.870
Every technology goes through
a sigmoidal adoption curve.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.050
5% of people are doing
it, then 10, then 15,

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.630
then suddenly it saturates
and goes vertical.

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:42.610
And only now in the last decade

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.750
or so have we had the
technology to make devices

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.120
that potentially could
last a decade or more.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.900
If we had a practical, permanent,
total artificial heart,

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.250
it would be one of the
biggest accomplishments

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.200
in modern medicine.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.870
When it comes to heart failure,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.860
there's currently only one
gold standard of treatment:

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.472
replacing the failing
heart with a healthy donor.

00:01:09.472 --> 00:01:12.480
And while there are nearly
6,000 heart transplants

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.470
done annually,

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.061
there are about a million requests

00:01:15.061 --> 00:01:16.820
for donor hearts each year,

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.373
600,000 of which are in the U.S. alone.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.250
So the main limitation
to heart transplantation

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.780
is having a donor heart that
we can place in a patient

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.710
who's destined to die from heart failure.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.870
Dr. William Cohn is a heart surgeon

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.660
at the Texas Heart Institute

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.778
and an innovator in
artificial heart technology.

00:01:35.778 --> 00:01:36.900
And if you think about it,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.820
it's gotta be a healthy heart

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.180
from a patient who's died in such way

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.307
that it didn't affect their heart.

00:01:42.307 --> 00:01:44.520
And that doesn't happen that frequently.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.740
So to be able to save the
lives of all these patients

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.180
that are dying of heart failure,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.680
we need something other
than a transplanted heart.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.100
Your heart's just a pump.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.320
Every cell in your body needs
nutrition, needs oxygen,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.510
it gets that through the blood

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.750
that goes through every
blood vessel in your body.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.828
The heart is the pump
that makes the blood move.

00:02:04.828 --> 00:02:09.828
So a heart is a very simple
device by some metrics,

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.874
but incredibly complex by others.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.230
So a lot of scientists looked

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.580
at the heart function
and tried to mimic it.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.670
The really first artificial heart

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:24.090
was designed by a guy
named Domingo Liotta,

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.370
brilliant Argentinian
scientist who was working

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:28.690
at the Cleveland clinic then came down

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.720
to work with Michael DeBakey,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.990
pioneering heart surgeon in Houston.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.470
And they developed a device that DeBakey

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.350
had implanted in seven cows.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:40.540
The longest went about 12 hours.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.300
While DeBakey was in Washington DC asking

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.540
for additional funding,

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.040
his then partner, Denton Cooley implanted

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.133
it in a desperate ill human
patient, Haskell Karp.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.440
April 4th, 1969,

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.980
that was the world's first clinical use

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.740
of a total artificial heart.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.960
The possibility has been established

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.634
as a reality, the fact that Mr. Karp

00:03:03.634 --> 00:03:08.634
has regained organ function
in terms of cerebral function

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.100
and kidney function indicates

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.593
that it's, a mechanical
heart is a feasibility.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:16.670
Unfortunately,

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.330
the valves that they'd put
in this version of the heart

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:20.830
were breaking up the blood,

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.670
the broken up blood was
causing the kidneys to fail,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.660
and after 64 hours on the device,

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.710
they did an emergency transplant

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.433
and tragically 36 hours
later died of an infection.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:37.283
But it showed us that that
type of thing was possible.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.910
The heart had bladders that filled

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.940
and emptied with compressed air

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:47.610
that drove the blood forward.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.880
One way valves made it so that
when the bladders collapsed,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.323
blood poured into the device,

00:03:52.323 --> 00:03:53.630
when the bladders filled,

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:56.120
it went out through the great vessels.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.830
Based on that technology,

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.015
that device continued
to evolve and evolved

00:04:00.015 --> 00:04:03.180
into what we now have,
the SynCardia device,

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.610
which is the world's
first approved device,

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.182
just like the one implanted in 1969,

00:04:08.182 --> 00:04:11.360
but much better membranes,
much better valves,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.250
much better material and
manufacturing techniques.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.180
Now we've just heard
that the Carmat device

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.580
has been approved for
commercial sale in Europe,

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:21.094
and that's great, that's a step forward,

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:24.823
but it's still approved for temporary use.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.460
SynCardia and Carmat

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.390
are the only approved artificial hearts

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:30.700
in the world right now.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.740
But they're currently
approved only as bridge

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:34.158
to transplant devices,

00:04:34.158 --> 00:04:37.260
temporary hearts that work
to keep patients alive

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.610
until a real donor
heart becomes available.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:43.050
The longest an artificial
heart has ever lasted

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:47.870
in a patient is 1,374 days
or roughly four years,

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:50.833
the real limitation being
long-term durability.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.750
Think about it now,

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:56.680
if your heart is beating
80 times a minute,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.050
that's 132,000 times in a day,

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:02.960
that's 42 million times in a year.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.080
So the challenge with
all the artificial hearts

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.218
that have been developed
so far is, it wears out.

00:05:07.218 --> 00:05:10.330
Anything that has to
flex over and over again

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:11.828
to make it so it never fails,

00:05:11.828 --> 00:05:13.543
I think this is a tall order.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.020
The durability factor has led other groups

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.370
to rethink their approach

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.922
to how an artificial heart mimics our own.

00:05:28.922 --> 00:05:31.380
BiVACOR, a startup from
Brisbane, Australia

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.430
that's headquartered in Houston Texas,

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.610
has come up with a device
that hardly resembles

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.700
a human heart at all.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.110
Their radical new approach
has helped them to raise more

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.590
than $20 million

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.720
and has won over industry
heavy hitters like Dr. Cohn,

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.420
who serves as their chief medical officer.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:48.853
My name is Daniel Timms,

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:50.682
I'm a mechanical engineer

00:05:50.682 --> 00:05:52.980
with a PhD in biomedical engineering.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.050
During my PhD,

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:57.670
I invented the BiVACOR device
and have been developing

00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:59.123
that device ever since.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:01.900
The artificial heart that we're creating

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.350
is what's called a rotary
total artificial heart,

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.040
which has a spinning disc,

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.930
which is magnetically
levitated within a cavity

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:11.390
to pump the blood to both the lungs

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:13.080
and to the body simultaneously.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.640
It's a similar process to a
fan where your ceiling fan

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.850
will spin and air will come
down at a constant flow rate.

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:22.090
Instead of air we're pumping blood.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:24.220
But it's very similar
impellers, effectively,

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:26.290
that's inside the artificial heart.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:28.110
And so that approach
is radically different

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.630
from utilizing compressing sacks.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.500
A spinning disc that is
magnetically levitated,

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:35.830
meaning that there's no touchdown of any

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:38.743
of those moving parts, can
affectively last forever.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:41.440
BiVACOR's artificial heart works off

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.560
of rapidly spinning pump technology,

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:46.700
developed by scientists
Rob Jarvik and Rich Wampler

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:48.550
in the mid 1980s.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:49.460
In their device,

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:52.330
small turbines sat
outside unhealthy hearts

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:55.730
to assist with blood flow
to a single ventricle.

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.950
Timms' device builds on that idea

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.630
by using a spinning disc, or impeller,

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:04.083
to push and regulate the flow
of blood to both ventricles.

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:06.220
The impeller inside,

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:09.970
it's a single disc with veins
on one side to pump the blood

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:11.750
to the body and veins on the other side,

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:12.930
to pump the blood to the lungs.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.090
And they look different because
the requirements of pressure

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.543
are different in the body and the lungs.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:20.590
The magnetic levitation system interfaces

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:22.070
with two ring shape magnets.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:25.020
That magnetic bearing detects this radar

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.090
and if the radar moves further away,

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:28.613
coils will energize to bring it closer.

00:07:28.613 --> 00:07:30.760
And if it gets too close,
they'll de-energize.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.460
And we'll do that 2000 times
a second to read the position

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:36.430
and then keep the disc levitated
well away from the casing,

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.830
therefore eliminating any
of the mechanical wear.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.470
BiVACOR is currently testing its device

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:50.295
in this system that circulates liquids

00:07:50.295 --> 00:07:52.943
to replicate the functions
of the human body.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:55.470
There's one big question, though,

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:58.410
that they won't be able to
answer before human trials:

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:01.600
whether BiVACOR patients
will have a pulse.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.750
While we tend to think of
the pulse as fundamental

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:04.840
to the heart's function,

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.190
BiVACOR's device can actually propel blood

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:09.130
in a continuous stream,

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:12.210
which works fine in the
lab and an animal trials,

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:14.190
but no one knows exactly what its effects

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:15.800
would be on the human body.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:18.560
So Timms is exploring
other options as well.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.060
Right now the device is operating

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:21.960
in a continuous flow mode.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:25.190
But then we want to introduce
pulsatility into that system.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:28.343
So we can turn on
pulsatility in that regard.

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:35.810
You'll see this device is
running in a pulsatile mode,

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:37.550
once every second.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:39.870
And we reproducing that
pulsatility by taking

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.910
the spinning disc and rapidly
spinning it up and down,

00:08:42.910 --> 00:08:44.490
up and down once a second.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.800
And that reproduces
that pulsatile outflow,

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:48.000
now that we can observe.

00:08:48.965 --> 00:08:51.440
What we then have the
ability for this device to do

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.160
is even change that beat rate.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:55.840
And where alternatively
the patient is exercising

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.560
and there might be a higher
beat rate required there.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.013
And this is at a 90 beats per minute now.

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:04.310
Resting and sleeping,

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:06.493
and so that pulse rate will be lower.

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:10.160
Eventually BiVACOR intends

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:11.490
for these changes in heart rate

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:14.670
to be automatic based on blood
pressure changes that occur

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:16.260
when a patient moves from one type

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.103
of physical activity to another.

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.910
This is a project that I've worked on now

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:29.663
for almost two decades.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:35.040
During my undergraduate
studies, around about that time,

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.823
my father had heart failure.

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:41.410
In the early days the whole goal

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:43.580
was to try to make
something, a little naively,

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:46.990
to help his failing circulation.

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:49.130
We decided that we'd do some of the work

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:51.400
in our garage or in our home.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:52.510
My father was a plumber.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:55.053
So he was very adept
with circulation systems.

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:58.940
So what we decided to
do was make a prototype

00:09:58.940 --> 00:09:59.973
of this device.

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:06.857
So this is one of the very
first prototype iterations

00:10:06.857 --> 00:10:08.640
of the BiVACOR device.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:09.740
So you'll see here,

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:13.120
we have an ability to put in
different horseshoe magnets,

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:17.100
on this side is where
the motor can fit inside,

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.250
then of course we look inside the pump

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:23.000
and we can see one of the
very first impeller designs

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:26.760
that was actually made by my dad.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:30.730
He machined this on a mini
lathe, right next to the TV.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:33.080
So he could work on the mini
lathe while watching TV.

00:10:35.051 --> 00:10:37.030
Although we are now creating a device

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:40.950
and obviously not in time for him,

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:42.770
there's many other people
out with heart failure

00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:45.700
and not only does it touch the patient

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:47.250
with the heart failure,

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:48.750
but their immediate family,

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:51.340
which is something that
my mum and and myself

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:55.070
have been dealing with now for
now 15 years since that time.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:57.850
So that really catalyzed

00:10:57.850 --> 00:10:59.780
the motivation and the passion really

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:03.493
to get this into those
patients that need it.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.610
BiVACOR is currently working with the FDA,

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:12.250
undergoing durability and animal studies.

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:14.500
They're also poised for
their first human implant

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:16.303
to take place in 2022.

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:21.500
Meanwhile, France based
Carmat received approval

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:23.810
from the EU at the end of 2020

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:25.240
and has started manufacturing

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:26.993
its hearts for commercial sale.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:30.770
Hopes are high that Carmat's
heart will prove longer lasting

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:32.407
than earlier artificial hearts,

00:11:32.407 --> 00:11:35.180
and the company intends for its
product to eventually become

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:38.163
a permanent replacement,
not just a stopgap.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:42.150
As promising as these technologies are,

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:43.640
one big remaining downside

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:45.800
is the briefcase-sized power packs

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.460
that have to stay permanently connected

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.441
to the hearts through the patient's skin.

00:11:49.441 --> 00:11:52.150
Advancements like extending battery life

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:53.750
and replacing cable drive lines

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:55.600
with wireless charging technology

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.090
would have to be realized,

00:11:57.090 --> 00:11:59.340
before an artificial
heart patient's freedom

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:02.533
is truly on par with a
healthy transplant recipient.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:07.440
You could argue that we're
still in this low phase,

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.040
we haven't gone vertical yet,

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.800
because there are four or
5,000 pumps implanted a year,

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:16.010
but 400,000 people dying of heart failure.

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:18.990
It's a whole bunch of technical forces

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:21.990
that are converging on
having an artificial heart

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:24.760
that lasts a long time,
that doesn't have a wire

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:25.670
through the skin.

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:27.910
And then, maybe heart failure

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:30.510
won't be the number one
cause of death in the world.

